      "role": "fs",
      "roleName": "Full-stack",
      "years": null,
      "text": "1) The ability to access any part of a system, because you'll still have to change the Environment Variables and turn the computer off and on again from time to time. 2) The ability to decide to start new projects. 3) The ability to actually be there physically in meetings, which helps read the room and understand when you'd do good to add certain features or change course, because your boss is subconsciously hinting at a corporate overhaul coming up. There's no way teams would consider it worth-while to add a video camera to meetings and have an AI process all that data just to pick up on non-verbal communication, so only humans will be able to be considerate of those things in their daily lives. Other than that, AI could be made to do basically anything - though I believe society will *choose* not to integrate AI to the extents that'd be needed for it to do so, and human developers will remain valuable precisely because we will still be allowed to have such reach over projects and systems."
